## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to translate product goals into a concrete cost model that accounts for on-device constraints, security requirements, and regulatory concerns. It also probes understanding of trade-offs for multi-app isolation, update mechanics, and privacy-by-design principles.

## Key Concepts
- On-device ML model footprints and TEE (Trusted Execution Environment) architecture
- Cross-app isolation with per-app policy overrides
- Telemetry budgeting and data minimization under privacy regulations
- OTA update economics, delta patching, and cryptographic key rotation
- Compliance frameworks (GDPR, CCPA), privacy-by-design, and break-even analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-break-even calculator
function needsGoNoGo(serverSavingsPerMonth, deviceCostsPerMonth, months) {
  const netSavings = serverSavingsPerMonth - deviceCostsPerMonth;
  const cumulativeSavings = netSavings * months;
  return {
    breakEvenMonths: deviceCostsPerMonth / netSavings,
    go: cumulativeSavings > 0 && months >= 18
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical CBA for a local-first search feature, balancing storage, CPU, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing
- Delta sync costs
- Privacy/compliance
- Update cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function indexRepo(files) {
  // naive tokenization
  const index = {};
  for (const f of files) {
    const tokens = f.content.split(/\\W+/).filter(Boolean);
    tokens.forEach(t => (index[t] ||= []).push(f.path));
  }
  return index;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure user time saved and energy impact to validate the break-even?
- How would you design for monorepos and very large repos with incremental indexing?

## Why This Is Asked

A practical UX feature tests offline resilience, local storage usage, and simple cross-tab conflict handling without server changes.

## Key Concepts

- Debounce autosave
- localStorage vs IndexedDB
- Versioning for conflict detection
- Cross-tab synchronization basics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal autosave scaffold
let timer;
function onChange(content){
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(()=> {
     const note = { content, lastSaved: Date.now(), version: (NOTE_VERSION++) };
     localStorage.setItem('noteDraft', JSON.stringify(note));
  }, 300);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test offline reload and cross-tab conflict?
- How would you handle storage quota and large notes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model privacy-preserving analytics, not ML, with real-world trade-offs across storage, compute, energy, and compliance.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy budgets & CRDTs for local aggregation
- Edge-resource constraints and telemetry design
- Compliance costs and regulatory penalties

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple CRDT-like Grow-Only Counter
class GCounter { constructor() { this.counters = new Map(); }
inc(k, v=1){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0)+v,0)); }
merge(other){ for(const [k,v] of other.counters){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0), v)); } }
value(){ let s=0; for(const v of this.counters.values()) s+=v; return s; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle CRDT reconciliation when offline and merging after sync?
- What metrics would you track to detect drift in your aggregates and how would you price the analytics feature?
